pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% table(x1,x2) in {(0,0),(1,1)} together with all_different(x1,x2) has no
% solution, but neither propagator sees that at the root.  It takes two nodes,
% and this is the whole proof, scaffolding included.
%
% Preamble: each variable takes at least one value.  Derived, not assumed.
@atleast1 pol @x1eq0_dn @x1eq1_dn + ;
@atleast2 pol @x2eq0_dn @x2eq1_dn + ;

% ---- node: decide x1 = 0 ----
% table: the tuple (1,1) is dead, so x2 = 1 has no support
rup x1eq0 ==> 1 ~x2eq1 >= 1 ;
% all_different: x1 and x2 are now both confined to {0}, a Hall violator.
% Adding the at-most-one row for value 0 to the two at-least-one rows cancels
% every atom for a value inside the violated set, and what is left says "one of
% x1, x2 takes a value outside {0}".  That row is true at the root; it is the
% node's own inferences that make it false here.
@hall_a pol @amo2_0 @atleast1 + @atleast2 + ;
% so the node fails: the decision that got us here leads to a contradiction,
% which is what an arrow with nothing on the right of it says
rup x1eq0 ==> >= 1 ;

% ---- node: decide x1 != 0, which leaves x1 = 1 ----
% table: symmetric, the tuple (0,0) is dead so x2 = 0 has no support
rup x1eq1 ==> 1 ~x2eq0 >= 1 ;
% all_different: both confined to {1} this time
@hall_b pol @amo2_1 @atleast1 + @atleast2 + ;
rup ~x1eq0 ==> >= 1 ;

% ---- both branches refuted ----
rup >= 1 ;
output NONE;
conclusion UNSAT;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
